Code copied from OpenStage Author: OpenScreen's capture engine (2026-11-14), from OpenStage Author
(https://github.com/jasonswrench-beep/openstage-author, main, commit 3c47a83ab4495e1b9f4a6f0cfaf996d4e4fbb1fc),
Apache License 2.0, Copyright 2026 Jason S. Wrench, the same owner:

- capture/crates/ (all six crates: openstage-capture, openstage-capture-cli, openstage-capture-project,
  openstage-capture-protocol, openstage-capture-render, openstage-capture-windows), capture/Cargo.toml,
  capture/Cargo.lock, capture/rustfmt.toml and capture/THIRD-PARTY.md. The code is unchanged; product names were
  taken out of a few comments and messages. capture/IMPORT-MANIFEST.md lists every file, its checksum and those changes. Studio uses the window capture, the MP4 writer
  (Windows' own H.264 and AAC encoders), the frame canvas and Windows' text drawing from it.
  The Rust crates built into the engine, each under its own permissive licence, are listed in capture/THIRD-PARTY.md.

Rust crates added by OpenStage Studio's own helper (studio-helper/Cargo.toml), beyond those the engine already uses:

- png (https://github.com/image-rs/image-png), MIT OR Apache-2.0, with its own dependencies
  (fdeflate, flate2, miniz_oxide, crc32fast, adler2, simd-adler32, bitflags), each MIT, Apache-2.0, Zlib or a choice of them.

The app's shell is Tauri (https://tauri.app), MIT OR Apache-2.0, with its dialog and file plugins, under the same licences.
A few of the crates Tauri brings in are under the Mozilla Public License 2.0 (file-level copyleft). They are used unchanged,
and their source is public:
- cssparser and cssparser-macros (https://github.com/servo/rust-cssparser)
- selectors (https://github.com/servo/stylo)
- dtoa-short (https://github.com/upsuper/dtoa-short)
- option-ext (https://github.com/soc/option-ext)

The app icon (branding/ and src-tauri/icons/) is OpenStage Studio's own, made for the OpenStage suite.

No GPL or AGPL code is used. Run "Check Licences.bat" to check every package and crate.

Narration with the Kokoro voice (studio-helper/src/kokoro.rs), added 2026-11-14:

- Kokoro-82M 1.0, ONNX build (https://huggingface.co/onnx-community/Kokoro-82M-v1.0-ONNX, revision
  1939ad2a8e416c0acfeecc08a694d14ef25f2231), Apache-2.0. Not bundled: downloaded on request by "Get Voice.bat", each file
  checked against its SHA-256 in tools/kokoro-model.json.
- ONNX Runtime 1.31.0 (https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime), MIT, linked into studio-helper through the ort crate
  (https://github.com/pykeio/ort, MIT OR Apache-2.0), whose build fetches the prebuilt library. Its licence and its own
  third-party notices are in third-party/onnxruntime/ and ship with the app. Among them, Eigen is under the Mozilla Public
  License 2.0 (file-level copyleft): it is used unchanged, and its source is at https://gitlab.com/libeigen/eigen. Accepted 2026-11-14.
- misaki-rs 0.9.0 (https://github.com/MicheleYin/misaki-rs), MIT, built without its default espeak feature, so no GPL code
  is included. It carries Kokoro's English pronunciation dictionaries from misaki (https://github.com/hexgrad/misaki),
  Apache-2.0, and a part-of-speech tagger whose weights were extracted from spaCy's en_core_web_sm model, MIT.
- language-tokenizer 0.1.0 (https://github.com/savannstm/language-tokenizer), a dependency of misaki-rs, under the WTFPL
  version 2: a public-domain-like licence with no conditions. Accepted 2026-11-14.

AI planning, scripting and rehearsal (src/ai/, src/manual/), added 2026-11-15:

- The built-in AI model: Qwen 3.5, 4 billion parameters, by Alibaba Cloud (Qwen), Apache-2.0, in the Q4_K_M file made by
  Unsloth (https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.5-4B-GGUF, revision e87f176479d0855a907a41277aca2f8ee7a09523). Not bundled:
  downloaded on request by "Set Up AI.bat", with its licence text, each file checked against its SHA-256 in
  tools/local-ai.json (the same pins the documentation tool uses).
- llama.cpp build b12359 (https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp), MIT, the program that runs the built-in model on this
  computer. Not bundled: downloaded with the model, checked the same way. Its zip carries nlohmann/json (MIT, licence
  downloaded beside it) and the LLVM OpenMP runtime (Apache-2.0 with LLVM Exceptions, licence kept with it).
- The Anthropic TypeScript SDK (@anthropic-ai/sdk, https://github.com/anthropics/anthropic-sdk-typescript), Apache-2.0,
  used only when a person chooses Claude with their own key. Its dependencies: standardwebhooks (MIT), json-schema-to-ts
  (MIT) and @opentelemetry/api (Apache-2.0).
- PDF.js (pdfjs-dist, https://github.com/mozilla/pdf.js), Apache-2.0, for reading the words of a PDF manual.
